solution:
In an equilateral triangle the altitude $AD$ is also a median and a perpendicular bisector, so the circumcentre $O$ lies on $AD$, and it coincides with the centroid. Hence $AO=\dfrac{2}{3}AD$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(6,0),A=(3,3*sqrt(3)),D=(3,0),O=(3,sqrt(3));
draw(circle(O,2*sqrt(3)),gray+linewidth(0.7));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,dashed);
draw(O--C,blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw((3,0)--(3,0.35)--(3.35,0.35)--(3.35,0),linewidth(0.5));
dot(O);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);label("$O$",O,W);
label("$6$",(A+B)/2,NW);
label("$R$",(O+C)/2,N,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Since $D$ is the midpoint of $BC$, $BD=3$ and by Pythagoras $AD=\sqrt{6^2-3^2}=3\sqrt3$. Therefore
\[
R=AO=\dfrac{2}{3}\cdot3\sqrt3=\boxed{2\sqrt3}.
\]
(Check: $OD=\sqrt3$ and $OC=\sqrt{OD^2+DC^2}=\sqrt{3+9}=2\sqrt3$.)

question:
Let $n$ be a positive integer, and let the last $n$ digits of a positive integer $N$ be $a_1a_2\ldots a_n$ (so $a_1$ is the digit in the $10^{n-1}$ place and $a_n$ is the units digit). A student proposes the following test:
\begin{quote}
$N$ is divisible by $2^n$ exactly when $n a_1+(n-1)a_2+(n-2)a_3+\dots+2a_{n-1}+a_n$ is divisible by $2^n$; and the same holds with $5^n$ in place of $2^n$.
\end{quote}
Prove or disprove the test. For which of the cases $2^1,2^2,5^1,5^2,2^3$ is it correct?

solution:
The probability is the area of the region of the rectangle between the circles $x^2+y^2=4$ and $x^2+y^2=15$, divided by the rectangle's area $15$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real r1=2, r2=sqrt(15);
pair O=(0,0), Q=(sqrt(6),3);
real th=degrees(atan(3/sqrt(6)));
path reg=arc(O,r2,0,th)--(0,3)--(0,r1)--arc(O,r1,90,0)--cycle;
fill(reg,lightgray);
draw((0,0)--(5,0)--(5,3)--(0,3)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(O,r1,0,90),linewidth(0.8));
draw(arc(O,r2,0,th),linewidth(0.8));
draw(O--Q,dashed);
dot(Q);
label("$O$",O,SW);label("$(5,0)$",(5,0),S);label("$(5,3)$",(5,3),NE);label("$(0,3)$",(0,3),W);
label("$Q(\sqrt6,3)$",Q,N);
label("$2$",(2,0),S);label("$\sqrt{15}$",(sqrt(15),0),S);
draw(arc(O,0.75,0,th),linewidth(0.5));
label("$\theta$",1.05*dir(th/2));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Inside the larger circle.} Since $\sqrt{15}<5$, the circle of radius $\sqrt{15}$ does not reach the side $x=5$, but it crosses the top side $y=3$ at $Q=(\sqrt6,3)$, because $6+9=15$. The part of the rectangle inside this circle is the right triangle with vertices $O$, $(0,3)$, $Q$, of area $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot3\cdot\sqrt6=\dfrac{3\sqrt6}{2}$, together with the circular sector between the $x$-axis and $OQ$, of angle $\theta=\arctan\dfrac{3}{\sqrt6}=\arctan\dfrac{\sqrt6}{2}$ and area $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot15\,\theta$.

\textbf{Inside the smaller circle.} The quarter disc of radius $2$ lies inside the rectangle (as $2<3$) and has area $\dfrac{1}{4}\cdot4\pi=\pi$.

So the shaded region has area $\dfrac{3\sqrt6}{2}+\dfrac{15}{2}\arctan\dfrac{\sqrt6}{2}-\pi$, and the probability is
\[
\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt6}{10}+\dfrac{1}{2}\arctan\dfrac{\sqrt6}{2}-\dfrac{\pi}{15}}\approx0.479 .
\]

Triangle $ADC$ has a right angle at $D$, so
\[
AD^2+DC^2=13^2=169,\qquad \dfrac{1}{2}\,AD\cdot DC=30\ \Longrightarrow\ AD\cdot DC=60 .
\]
Then $(AD+DC)^2=169+120=289$ and $(AD-DC)^2=169-120=49$, so $AD+DC=17$, $|AD-DC|=7$, and $\{AD,DC\}=\{12,5\}$.

\textbf{Case $AD=12$, $DC=5$.} In right triangle $ADB$, $BD=\sqrt{15^2-12^2}=9$. In an acute triangle the foot of an altitude lies between the other two vertices, so $BC=BD+DC=14$ and
\[
[ABC]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot14\cdot12=84 .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(14,0),A=(9,12),D=(9,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,linewidth(0.8));
draw((9,0)--(9,0.7)--(8.3,0.7)--(8.3,0),linewidth(0.5));
fill(A--D--C--cycle,lightgray+opacity(0.5));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));draw(A--D,linewidth(0.8));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);
label("$15$",(A+B)/2,NW);label("$13$",(A+C)/2,NE);
label("$12$",(9,6),W);
label("$9$",(4.5,0),S);label("$5$",(11.5,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Case $AD=5$, $DC=12$.} Then $BD=\sqrt{225-25}=10\sqrt2$, and the area $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot5\,(10\sqrt2+12)=30+25\sqrt2$ is not an integer. (This triangle, with $BC=10\sqrt2+12$, is not even acute: $15^2+13^2=394<BC^2$.)

Hence the area of triangle $ABC$ is $\boxed{84}$; it is the $13$-$14$-$15$ triangle.

Let $n$ have a proper divisor, and let $p$ be its smallest prime factor. Then the smallest proper divisor is $p$ and the largest is $\dfrac {n}{p}$. The condition $\dfrac {n}{p}=45p$ gives $n=45p^2=3^2\cdot5\cdot p^2$.

For $p$ to be the smallest prime factor of $n$ we need $p\le3$, since $3\mid n$. Both values work:
\begin{itemize}
\item $p=2$: $n=180$, smallest proper divisor $2$, largest $90=45\cdot2$;
\item $p=3$: $n=405$, smallest proper divisor $3$, largest $135=45\cdot3$.
\end{itemize}
So there are exactly two such numbers: $\boxed{\textbf{(C)}\ 2}$.

\textbf{$A$.} With $y=\dfrac{1}{3}$: $3x+3=2$, so $x=-\dfrac{1}{3}$ and $A=\left(-\dfrac{1}{3},\dfrac{1}{3}\right)$.

\textbf{$B$.} With $x=\dfrac{2}{3}$: $2+9y=2$, so $y=0$ and $B=\left(\dfrac{2}{3},0\right)$.

\textbf{Slope.} Through $A$ and $B$,
\[
m=\dfrac{0-\dfrac{1}{3}}{\dfrac{2}{3}-\left(-\dfrac{1}{3}\right)}=\dfrac{-\dfrac{1}{3}}{1}=\boxed{-\dfrac{1}{3}}.
\]
(Equivalently, $y=-\dfrac{1}{3}x+\dfrac{2}{9}$.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return (2-3x)/9;}
draw((-1.7,0)--(1.4,0),Arrow(6));draw((0,-0.45)--(0,0.8),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(1.4,0),E);label("$y$",(0,0.8),N);
draw(graph(f,-1.6,1.3),blue+linewidth(0.9));
pair A=(-1/3,1/3),B=(2/3,0);
dot(A);dot(B);
draw((-1/3,0)--A--(0,1/3),dashed+gray);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$-\dfrac{1}{3}$",(-1/3,0),S);draw((0,1/3)--(0.04,1/3));label("$\dfrac{1}{3}$",(0.03,1/3),dir(20),fontsize(9pt));
label("$B$",B,SW);label("$\dfrac{2}{3}$",(2/3,0),2*N);
label("$3x+9y=2$",(1.3,f(1.3)),SE,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}
